  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The brown-net bolete is a medium-sized fungus characterized by its brown, reticulated (net-like) stem pattern and pinkish pores that bruise blue-green when handled. It is a bolete species found in the forests of New England and is inedible due to its bitter taste.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Reticulated Stem",
      "description": "Distinctive net-like or honeycomb pattern on the stem surface, typically brown on a lighter background",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Color",
      "description": "Pores are pale pink to rosy, becoming darker and bruising blue-green when touched or damaged",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Appearance",
      "description": "Brown to grayish-brown cap, often with a matte or slightly wrinkled surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Brown-net boletes are typically found on the forest floor in hardwood and mixed forests throughout New England, fruiting from summer through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The blue-green bruising reaction observed when the pores are damaged is caused by a chemical reaction between compounds in the fungal tissue and oxygen in the air, making it a useful field identification clue.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
